WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
MUSIC

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:25.000
SINGING

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.000
The island of Komodo in Indonesia.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Remote and barren.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
MUSIC

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Few people live here.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
It's ruled by a giant reptile.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:06.000
MUSIC

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The Komodo dragon.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Nine feet long.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
A top predator.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Only one thing can challenge a dragon's dominance.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Another dragon.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.000
In the breeding season, males are drawn into savage conflict.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Huge claws and 60 serrated teeth can inflict terrible damage.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000
MUSIC

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.000
The dragon's teeth are very sharp.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:23.000
MUSIC

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The Komodo dragon is the only reptile that still rules the land, as dinosaurs once did.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:53.000
It can dominate here because it has no competition.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.000
But for the rest of the world's reptiles and amphibians, survival is a much tougher struggle.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:12.000
MUSIC

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Like the dragon, reptiles and amphibians seem primitive, better suited to an earlier age.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
MUSIC

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Yet they have a surprising repertoire of extraordinary strategies.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
And they still thrive in many parts of the planet.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.000
This is the story of how they do so.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:58.000
MUSIC

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.000
South America.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
The remote jungle where Brazil and Venezuela meet.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Shear-sided mountains rise up from the jungle.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Few places on Earth are wetter.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.000
It's the home of some ancient and very peculiar amphibians.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.000
The waterfall toad is just an inch long, the size of a postage stamp.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Its special gripping hands enable it to clamber safely high in the treetops.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:58.000
MUSIC

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
But it's not alone up here.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Most frogs and toads avoid predators by hopping out of danger.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.000
But this toad never evolved the ability to hop for more than an inch.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.000
And that's nowhere near enough.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.000
One way it gets eaten.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.000
The other way is thin air.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Not much of a choice.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:44.000
MUSIC

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.000
But this isn't suicide.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.000
It's a deliberate strategy and one that depends on the size and strength of those extraordinary hands.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Free falling like this is a very effective way of escaping.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.000
MUSIC

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
However, another toad on this same mountain has elaborated this strategy.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.000
MUSIC

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.000
It lives a mile above the forest on this plateau, cut off by a giant tree.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.000
A forest on this plateau, cut off from the world below.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:36.000
MUSIC

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
This is a pebble toad.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
It too is only an inch long.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:48.000
MUSIC

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.000
These rocks may seem a paradise for a toad.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.000
It's even wetter than the forest below and there are no snakes.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.000
MUSIC

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.000
But there is a hunter here.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:21.000
MUSIC

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.000
A toad-eating tarantula.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:31.000
MUSIC

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.000
It ambushes its prey.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:41.000
MUSIC

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:47.000
Like the waterfall toad, the pebble toad can't hop, but it has a different defence.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:08:06.000
MUSIC

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.000
It tenses its muscles, becomes rigid and turns itself into a rubber ball.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:20.000
MUSIC

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.000
It's so tiny and weighs so little that bouncing doesn't hurt it at all.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:34.000
MUSIC

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:41.000
MUSIC

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:48.000
Bizarre innovations like these are one of the reasons why amphibians and reptiles are still so successful.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:56.000
MUSIC

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:03.000
BUBBLING

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:11.000
Some of them, however, have hardly changed for tens of millions of years.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:21.000
BUBBLING

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.000
Brazil's Pantanal.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Over 50,000 square miles of swamp.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:36.000
In the dry season, the water is reduced to isolated pools.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:42.000
And then they contain the greatest concentration of crocodiles on Earth.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.000
THUNDER RUMBLES

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.000
These caiman crocodiles are trapped and fast running out of food.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:58.000
It will be a long time before the rains return and the rivers rise, bringing with them the caiman's prey, fish.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.000
THUNDER RUMBLES

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Being cold-blooded, they can go without food for months and stay inactive, except when they squabble.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:27.000
THUNDER RUMBLES

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:37.000
THUNDER RUMBLES

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:48.000
THUNDER RUMBLES

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.000
At last, the rains return and rivers swell.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:11:08.000
MUSIC

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.000
All reptiles are experts at saving energy.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.000
The caiman simply line up and wait for the fish to come to them.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:46.000
MUSIC

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:56.000
THUNDER RUMBLES

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:02.000
Reptiles and amphibians must warm their bodies before they can become active.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.000
And until then, they are slow-moving and vulnerable.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:18.000
MUSIC

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:26.000
The quickest way to gain heat is to bask in the sun, as this basilisk lizard from Central America is doing.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.000
The trouble is that exposing yourself inevitably makes you easily seen.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:35.000
BIRDS CHIRP

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:44.000
But a conspicuous perch over a river like this seems to be making yourself unnecessarily prominent.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.000
This basilisk's greatest threat comes from the sky.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:55.000
Dark shapes overhead make it nervous.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:03.000
A hunting bird might expect it to flee to the trees.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:09.000
In fact, it does the opposite.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:17.000
This skitter across the surface of the water earns it precious seconds and the nickname Jesus Christ Lizard.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:25.000
MUSIC

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:32.000
It drives its feet down so hard and so fast that it never sinks deeper than a few inches.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.000
A human would need to run at 65 miles an hour to do this.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:14:06.000
MUSIC

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:21.000
BIRDS CHIRP

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.000
Reptiles are certainly expert at avoiding trouble,

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:31.000
but they're also very good at finding a way to live in places that don't seem to suit them at all.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:43.000
BIRDS CHIRP

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:51.000
They once included the largest animals that have ever walked the Earth, the dinosaurs.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:15:01.000
And some living ones are a million times smaller than T. rex, smaller indeed than some insects.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:10.000
BIRDS CHIRP

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:16.000
The Brazilian pygmy gecko could sit comfortably on a fingertip.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:22.000
It's so exceptionally small that no other reptiles compete with it.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:28.000
But it's so nimble it can escape from the hunting monsters of the undergrowth.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:38.000
BIRDS CHIRP

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:55.000
It's so small that it has special problems with, for example, rain.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:16:02.000
It could drown in a drop, but its skin is hydrophobic.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:07.000
Rain cannot wet it.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:14.000
A tumble into a puddle, you might think, would risk death by drowning.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:24.000
MUSIC

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:30.000
But the gecko is unsinkable.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:45.000
It's so light and its skin so water-repellent that it can literally stand on water.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:53.000
Such militarization is certainly very impressive, but the reptile body can be transformed even more dramatically.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:17:01.000
MUSIC

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:14.000
One of the most bizarre of all reptiles hunts insects here in Madagascar.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:42.000
The praying mantis has a 360-degree vision, but it hasn't seen the danger that is now approaching.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:53.000
A panther chameleon, every part of its body has become specialized for a life of stealth among the branches.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:59.000
Its toes have become grasping pincers.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:05.000
Its eyes rotate so it can look in all directions.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Its skin can change color for camouflage or to signal emotions.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:43.000
The chameleon body is concealed from both predator and prey so it can launch a surprise attack.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:19:07.000
The tongue is like a missile, aimed at its prey's head to neutralize its defenses.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:34.000
Chameleons, in short, are so perfectly adapted to a life spent hunting in trees that it's hard to imagine how they could live anywhere else.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:42.000
The Namib Desert, there are no trees for hundreds of miles.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:51.000
Yet these are undoubtedly chameleon footprints.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:58.000
This female Namakwa chameleon is searching for a mate.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:11.000
No part of her body seems suited to such an environment.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:23.000
Her feet, that could give her such a good grip on twigs, should surely be useless on soft sand, but she's able to spread them like snowshoes.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:30.000
She needs to get warm and active while the desert is still cool, so she exploits the chameleon's versatile skin.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:50.000
The side facing the sun goes dark to absorb the sun's heat, while the other remains light and minimizes the heat escaping from her body.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:21:17.000
She's hungry and food is scarce, but she's still not quick enough to grab these desert beetles.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:46.000
Her solution is simple. She finds, at last, a little vegetation and waits for a shade-seeking beetle to come to her.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:22:14.000
On she goes in her search for a mate.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:39.000
At last, a wandering male. But he may not treat her as she might wish.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:23:08.000
The courtship of most chameleons is gentle, but in this desert a male meets a female so rarely he can't risk being rejected.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:36.000
She marches on, now carrying a new generation.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:49.000
A six-month freeze, when temperatures may drop to minus 40 degrees centigrade, should surely make things impossible for a reptile.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:24:09.000
But here in Canada, beneath the thawing snow, something stirs. A male red-sided garter snake. He survived the winter by hibernating underground, where the temperature never dropped below zero.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:37.000
The weak sun persuades more males to emerge. They're cold and can't move fast, yet they are in an urgent race. The first males to warm up will have a head start when the first females appear.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:25:06.000
Meltwater provides the first drink they've had for six months.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:35.000
At last, a female has emerged. The warmest males will inevitably be the first to react to her smell. She will only mate once, so competition between them is intense.

00:25:35.000 --> 00:26:02.000
This male has overslept. He will need hours to warm up. At the moment, he stands no chance of mating.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:18.000
Most of the other males are ready to chase females, but curiously, some leave the race and go to join the cold male.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:30.000
They slide their warm bodies over him, just as they would if they were courting a female.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:47.000
More and more males crowd around him. Why? Their relative temperatures show what's going on.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:27:05.000
His cool body, showing us blue, is quickly warming as it absorbs heat from the other males. He's a trickster. He's fooled the others by giving off a scent just like a female's, and they are trying to mate with him.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:13.000
He only needs a few minutes of this to steal enough heat from his rivals to catch up and join the chase.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:26.000
Every spring, tens of thousands of garter snakes fight it out in this mating frenzy.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:49.000
It is, in numbers, the greatest gathering of reptiles in the world.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:57.000
Getting a mate is just the first challenge a reptile faces when it's ready to breed.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:12.000
This collard iguana in Madagascar will only lay one batch of eggs this year, so she needs somewhere safe to hide them.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:21.000
Her eggs are much sought after by predators. They're excellent pre-packaged meals.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:47.000
She appears to have the answer.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:56.000
She covers all traces of where she buried them. It's a good strategy.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:02.000
But not good enough.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:18.000
Hog-nosed snakes have learned to keep watch on iguana nesting sites and to note the precise place where the eggs lie buried.

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:34.000
She has been completely outwitted.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:30:01.000
She can only stand by and watch as her precious eggs are eaten. Next year, she'll have to find a nesting site without a hog-nosed snake.

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:22.000
On the other side of the world, in the Sonoran Desert of Arizona, a similar drama has a very different outcome.

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:37.000
A female horned lizard guards her buried eggs and is keeping a lookout for anything that might interfere with them.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:56.000
A western patch-nosed snake. It is an egg eater. And she won't stand for it.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:21.000
The patch-nosed is no match for this kind of aggression.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:32.000
Her eggs are safe for now.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:45.000
Another predator, a coach-whip snake. And this species is a lizard eater. And she can tell the difference.

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:53.000
The snake is too fast for her to outrun it, so she makes herself look larger and taller.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:02.000
The snake is wary of swallowing anything that looks as big and as spiky as this.

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:13.000
And now the lizard plays her trump card.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:38.000
The snake isn't accustomed to prey that does this.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:51.000
One very confused snake.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:33:11.000
Now she can return to guard duty. And several months later, she's rewarded with her first baby.

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:19.000
Looking after your eggs is an even greater challenge for reptiles that have left land to live in the sea.

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:29.000
The Pacific island of Niue is home to the world's entire population of one particular type of sea-crate.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:37.000
This snake is superbly adapted to life underwater, except when it's time to breed.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:45.000
After a few minutes in a mating coil, the female is fertilized. But now she has a problem.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:55.000
Reptile eggs can't survive underwater. The developing young would drown. They have to be able to breathe air.

00:33:55.000 --> 00:34:01.000
She and her eggs are both vulnerable to predators if they return to land.

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:13.000
But she has an extraordinary solution.

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:41.000
She dives beneath the island to find the entrance to a long underwater tunnel.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:35:05.000
At the far end is a cave with an air pocket.

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:16.000
This is the dry land her eggs need. There are no predators here.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:38.000
The cavern is sealed by a roof above and the water below. She searches for the crevice where she can deposit her eggs.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:36:06.000
The eggs will be completely safe here and she can return to the open sea.

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:31.000
Six months later, a baby snake takes its first breath.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:50.000
Somehow, the young snakes must find their own way to water.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:37:04.000
Then down the tunnel and out to sea.

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:35.000
A male giant bullfrog.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:47.000
Reptiles and amphibians rarely care for their young after they hatch, but this South African bruiser is an exception.

00:37:47.000 --> 00:38:00.000
The bullfrogs sought out their dominance through combat. The strongest will mate with the most females.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:18.400
This Nation opposition is exposed to pronto- successful

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:44.760
You might not expect such an aggressive male to be a caring father.

00:38:44.760 --> 00:39:06.320
But one male stays behind to watch over not just his own, but everyone else's offspring.

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:17.120
The females laid their eggs in the shallows around the margins of the pool.

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:26.160
But the sun is intense and the shallows have shrunk to a single pool.

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:30.920
There's a real danger that the tadpoles will be stranded.

00:39:30.920 --> 00:39:41.160
The guardian male recognizes the problem.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:57.360
They will be dead within an hour unless he can do something to help.

00:39:57.360 --> 00:40:05.760
There is only one thing he can do to save them.

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:33.280
Dig a channel to the main pool.

00:40:35.760 --> 00:41:04.760
They're safe.

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:13.400
Remarkable innovations have made amphibians and reptiles a modern success story.

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:22.560
What is more, among their ranks is one reptile that is still an undisputed top land predator.

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:25.800
The Komodo dragon.

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:31.280
They mostly hunt deer, which they ambush and easily overpower.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:39.080
But in hard times, the dragons risk hunting something much bigger.

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:50.800
Water buffalo, an animal ten times their size.

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:59.040
Until now, a dragon hunt has never been filmed.

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:00.760
It's the dry season.

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:10.000
A buffalo sleeps through the heat of the day in one of the last water holes.

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:13.360
Dragons lurk around the margins.

00:42:13.360 --> 00:42:21.240
The buffalo seems to view them as just an irritation, not a danger.

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:34.640
A serious mistake.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:36.280
The dragon is wary.

00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:57.840
A jab or a kick could injure it fatally.

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:15.160
The bites are just flesh wounds, but other dragons are alert now.

00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:29.000
Like sharks, they're excited by blood.

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:36.320
The buffalo leaves with just a limp.

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:46.360
The dragons appear to have failed.

00:43:46.360 --> 00:44:02.720
Yet they show a peculiar interest in the buffalo and follow it wherever it goes.

00:44:02.720 --> 00:44:07.680
As the days pass, the buffalo's wounds don't heal.

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:09.800
It starts to weaken.

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:14.840
The dragon's hunting method begins to come clear.

00:44:14.840 --> 00:44:21.160
A brand new discovery reveals that the dragon has venom, like a snake.

00:44:21.160 --> 00:44:36.640
The bite will eventually prove fatal, but it's going to take several weeks.

00:44:36.640 --> 00:44:43.160
The dragons, however, can afford to wait for a meal of this size.

00:44:43.160 --> 00:44:48.680
They simply stick close to their victim and conserve their energy.

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:54.480
Thousands of years ago, dwarf elephants lived here.

00:44:54.480 --> 00:45:00.800
The dragons probably hunted them in exactly the same way.

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:04.880
But the process is a long drawn out one.

00:45:04.880 --> 00:45:11.920
Three weeks later, and the buffalo is very weak.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:40.440
The dragon says that the end is near.

00:45:40.440 --> 00:45:46.560
They must remain wary.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:56.200
The buffalo could still lash out.

00:45:56.200 --> 00:46:17.760
Gradually they close in to try and finish it off.

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:38.720
The dragon's strategy is certainly merciless, but it is the only way that they can get a

00:46:38.720 --> 00:47:00.560
meal like this, and in hard times they need it if they're to survive.

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:09.120
The buffalo died in the night.

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:17.520
Ten big dragons have gathered to feed.

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:30.420
They stripped the buffalo to the bone in just four hours.

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:55.200
This is the biggest venomous animal on the planet.

00:47:55.200 --> 00:48:01.960
We don't need tales of Jurassic Park and velociraptors to see a reptile dominated

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:04.160
world.

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:23.460
It's all here.

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:35.160
No one has ever before followed the whole process of a Komodo dragon hunting a buffalo.

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:40.900
So cameraman Kevin Fley and researcher Matt Swarbrick didn't know what to expect.

00:48:40.900 --> 00:48:46.480
They thought it would be a physical challenge, but they hadn't bargained for emotional turmoil

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:57.200
as well.

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:00.060
They've reached Komodo after a four day journey.

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:08.860
Kevin, an expert at filming reptiles, knows they're in dragon territory.

00:49:08.860 --> 00:49:22.960
Their only protection is these rangers armed with sticks.

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:28.600
These big ones look really scary actually, and not until they come really close and when

00:49:28.600 --> 00:49:45.540
they're by the camera, then you tend to question your faith in our friends with the sticks.

00:49:45.540 --> 00:49:50.320
But this dragon isn't a big one.

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:54.040
At five foot, this dragon is just over half grown.

00:49:54.040 --> 00:50:00.080
The really big ones live inland, which is exactly where Kevin and Matt must go to find

00:50:00.080 --> 00:50:07.920
a place where dragons and buffalo might meet.

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:12.580
We could spend five weeks walking around all over the island just trying to find dragons

00:50:12.580 --> 00:50:14.720
randomly coming across buffaloes.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:21.120
But fortunately for us, it is the dry season and there's only one decent water hole left

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:26.720
on the island, and both the dragons and the buffalo require water, obviously, which brings

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:28.760
them together.

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:34.680
Kevin decides to wait here, but after a few days with no sign of dragons, it looks like

00:50:34.680 --> 00:50:35.680
the wrong decision.

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:36.680
Here comes a buffalo.

00:50:36.680 --> 00:50:42.000
You know, this is about as exciting as the day gets.

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:43.400
Look.

00:50:43.400 --> 00:50:46.040
See that?

00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:49.280
Hello mate.

00:50:49.280 --> 00:50:58.120
After almost a week, something changes and a couple of dragons appear.

00:50:58.120 --> 00:51:03.160
We've got a ranger here.

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:08.400
Guys, can we have a ranger?

00:51:08.400 --> 00:51:17.320
Always know how dangerous the situation is, but it does seem a bit concerning that one

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:26.000
of our rangers has set up at a tree.

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:33.160
But the dragons show no interest in the buffalo or the crew, and the frustration continues.

00:51:33.160 --> 00:51:36.920
Yet their task seems so simple.

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:44.600
So all we need is for him to film him attacking him.

00:51:44.600 --> 00:51:57.160
But it looks like he is about to attack him.

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:58.160
Just cut myself.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:51:59.160
That's not good in dragon country.

00:51:59.160 --> 00:52:00.160
I better be careful.

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:01.160
Now we've got to start our walk home.

00:52:01.160 --> 00:52:09.720
It takes just over an hour, but the thing is, if stuff's happened and you've managed

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:11.960
to film good stuff, then it's really easy.

00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:16.320
Just trot on home and find some dinner and find out what the rats have done to your room.

00:52:16.320 --> 00:52:20.480
But it's still really hot at this time of day, and so if you've not filmed a lot and

00:52:20.480 --> 00:52:24.840
you just sat here all day long, then it's a hell of a long walk home.

00:52:24.840 --> 00:52:30.320
They have a lot of long walks home, but finally a new dragon appears.

00:52:30.320 --> 00:52:34.840
Something about its attitude seems different.

00:52:34.840 --> 00:52:38.920
Maybe he could be onto something, but I've now lost that other dragon, which makes me

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:45.040
a bit nervous.

00:52:45.040 --> 00:52:53.320
At first it's wary, unwilling to get too close to the buffalo.

00:52:53.320 --> 00:53:04.880
Big dragons have walked in.

00:53:04.880 --> 00:53:11.160
That came from absolutely nowhere.

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:24.840
That guy has got some real attitude.

00:53:24.840 --> 00:53:40.360
That first bite and the smell of blood set off a chain reaction.

00:53:40.360 --> 00:53:48.720
All the dragons seem to come to life.

00:53:48.720 --> 00:53:55.240
The peaceful watering hole is now a dangerous place to be.

00:53:55.240 --> 00:54:02.320
I've definitely got two heads at the moment.

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:05.920
One has a cameraman saying, yes, brilliant.

00:54:05.920 --> 00:54:08.000
And the other one's thinking, poor animal.

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:09.000
You know, that's not nice.

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:10.000
That's really not nice.

00:54:10.000 --> 00:54:37.800
Heart's pumping a bit.

00:54:37.800 --> 00:54:41.440
At least my heart still works.

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:46.480
Oh, Lord, that was a bit hairy.

00:54:46.480 --> 00:54:51.640
Now the crew must follow the buffalo every day to finish their filming job.

00:54:51.640 --> 00:54:54.600
This begins to take a toll on their emotions.

00:54:54.600 --> 00:55:01.800
He knows he's injured, he can't walk properly, and he's got these predators just gathering

00:55:01.800 --> 00:55:04.800
around and waiting for the opportunity.

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:06.800
It's gruesome.

00:55:06.800 --> 00:55:12.200
Shattering the buffalo means staying close to the dragons too.

00:55:12.200 --> 00:55:13.880
This is mad.

00:55:13.880 --> 00:55:20.520
We've just followed the buffalo and I think six dragons.

00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:21.520
I've lost track now.

00:55:21.520 --> 00:55:24.520
I think it's six dragons down into the river bed.

00:55:24.520 --> 00:55:27.160
And they're stalking it.

00:55:27.160 --> 00:55:29.160
We're all a bit nervous.

00:55:29.160 --> 00:55:40.520
It's actually quite frightening, I have to say.

00:55:40.520 --> 00:55:47.840
They're so capable and so unafraid of us and they would have no problems at all causing

00:55:47.840 --> 00:55:51.760
us major, major damage.

00:55:51.760 --> 00:55:57.840
At the moment, I feel about five to 10% confident that I'm safe here.

00:55:57.840 --> 00:56:10.840
The truth is, we just don't know.

00:56:10.840 --> 00:56:19.360
As the days pass, Kevin and Matt can't help but feel more deeply involved.

00:56:19.360 --> 00:56:22.880
And I'm sure they're starting to think of us as death, you know, this camera team, because

00:56:22.880 --> 00:56:27.880
although we're not influencing it in any way really, we're always there when the dragon's

00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:31.360
there and she's sick so we're following her.

00:56:31.360 --> 00:56:36.200
And you see her looking back at you and I'm sure I feel like death on his side is coming

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:38.200
in to kill.

00:56:38.200 --> 00:56:42.800
I'm not really that sure how cut out I am for this.

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:52.840
If the game is spending all your time watching an animal die, I don't know about that.

00:56:52.840 --> 00:57:06.640
The dragons seem brutal, yet this is the only way they can get large prey.

00:57:06.640 --> 00:57:11.080
For the team, the event has become personal.

00:57:11.080 --> 00:57:15.040
I think because we've got to know him, you know, we've seen him every morning, we've

00:57:15.040 --> 00:57:20.600
seen him every evening and you can, you know, you build a relationship with him.

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:29.280
And today I think he kind of gave up.

00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:34.480
The next day, it is all over.

00:57:34.480 --> 00:57:46.640
Now it's over, you sit back and reflect, you sort of feel that was just astonishing.

00:57:46.640 --> 00:57:50.680
And it feels so primeval.

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:56.600
It feels you're watching something from a different era and they really are the most

00:57:56.600 --> 00:57:59.200
fantastic things to be with.

00:57:59.200 --> 00:58:06.080
Scary, awesome, you know, to look at you just feel that you are looking at something from

00:58:06.080 --> 00:58:17.360
Jurassic Park.
